{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789055045,"nonce":"kW7Ca0pkm6gHyo+7GTpCSFfp5Xg=","message":"CORRECTION, and the cause. My earlier 'ruled out: caused by sqlite.wasm now existing' was wrong -- I dismissed it on ONE passing run against a failure rate near 1 in 60, which is no evidence at all.\n\nThe correlation is exact. Across five CI runs, threads/wasm-fibers and the sqlite side module flip together:\n\n    8f861b0   sqlite.wasm absent                 threads/fibers PASS\n    297fa56   build attempted, failed (no rust-src)  threads/fibers PASS\n    1766e81   sqlite.wasm BUILT                  threads/fibers FAIL\n    70c4b2d   built                              threads/fibers FAIL\n    06f09eb   built                              threads/fibers FAIL\n\nAnd on the NUC, powered this time:\n\n    60 runs WITHOUT bin/sqlite.wasm present:  0 failures\n    60 runs WITH it present:                  1 failure\n\nCI fails it every run with the module present; the NUC about one in sixty. A loaded box changes the timing, which fits a race.\n\n**It is a LOAD-time interaction, not a build-time one.** Building threads.hl with and without sqlite.wasm sitting beside it produces BYTE-IDENTICAL modules (5,345,737 bytes, cmp clean). So this is not hdll_imports pinning exports or changing the suspend set -- I guessed that first and it is wrong. What changes is that the program then loads the side module at run time; the first line of the failing run is:\n\n    [ash] loaded native library: sqlite\n\n**So nothing regressed in ash.** Fibers and side modules had never run together before: the sys suite exercises sqlite without fibers, the threads suite exercises fibers without a side module, and the conformance job never built a side module until 1766e81. The combination is what is broken, and it was always broken.\n\nWhere to look next, given that: a dylink side module imports __memory_base and __table_base from the main module and takes a slice of its linear memory and function table. The fiber transform saves live locals into a linear-memory region the guest owns (handed to the host through ash_host_fiber_arm) -- whether that region can overlap what the side module is given, or whether a saved table index survives the table growing, is the question. crates/ash_wasm_link/src/dylink.rs and fiber.rs.\n\nStill ruled out, with measurements that were actually powered: GC rooting (ASH_GC_STRESS=1, 0 of 60 against 1 of 60 without), a stale-module rebuild, and CI-specificity.","files":null}]}